<!-- MAIN -->
<main id="content">

  <!-- CALLBACK TO ISSUE 01 -->
  <div class="callback">
    <div class="callback-icon">↩</div>
    <div class="callback-body">
      <div class="callback-eyebrow">A quick callback · Issue 01</div>
      <p>In Issue 01, we introduced the stress response: the amygdala fires an alert, cortisol and adrenaline flood your system, and your brain enters fight-or-flight mode. We established that <strong>chronic stress is harmful</strong> because it keeps that alarm running long after the threat is gone. This issue picks up right there and adds a new layer — what happens when sugar enters that already stressed brain?</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- WHAT IS IT -->
  <section>
    <div class="section-label">What is it</div>
    <h2>Sugar, Stress, and Your Brain Are in a Loop</h2>
    <p>When you are stressed, your body craves sugar. This is not a personality flaw. It is biology. <strong>Cortisol</strong>, the primary stress hormone we covered in Issue 01, signals your liver to release glucose into the bloodstream for quick energy. At the same time, it triggers cravings for high-calorie, high-sugar foods because your brain interprets stress as a physical emergency that requires fast fuel.</p>
    <div class="pull-quote">
      Eating sugar provides a brief feeling of relief because it temporarily raises serotonin and dopamine — two brain chemicals linked to mood and reward. Your brain registers this as: <em>sugar helped.</em> So the next time stress hits, it reaches for the same solution.
    </div>
    <p>Over time, this becomes a cycle. Stress triggers sugar cravings, sugar provides momentary relief, cortisol spikes again after the sugar crash, and stress returns — often worse than before.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- WHY YOU SHOULD CARE -->
  <section>
    <div class="section-label">Why You Should Care</div>
    <h2>The Crash After the Sugar High Is Making Your Stress Worse</h2>
    <p>When you eat something high in sugar, your blood glucose shoots up quickly. Your pancreas responds by releasing insulin to bring it back down. But in teens, this response can be sharp — causing blood sugar to drop <em>below baseline</em>. This is the crash. And during a crash, your brain perceives it as a threat, triggering another release of cortisol.</p>
    <p>In other words: eating sugar under stress does not resolve the stress. It <strong>delays it, and then amplifies it</strong>. Research from the University of California found that chronic high sugar intake keeps cortisol elevated even during periods of rest. Teens who regularly consumed sugary drinks showed higher baseline stress hormone levels compared to those who did not — even when their life circumstances were similar.</p>

    <!-- LOOP DIAGRAM -->
    <div class="loop-diagram">
      <div class="loop-eyebrow">The loop in plain terms</div>
      <div class="loop-steps">
        <div class="loop-node">Stress</div>
        <div class="loop-arrow">→</div>
        <div class="loop-node">Cortisol rises</div>
        <div class="loop-arrow">→</div>
        <div class="loop-node">Sugar craving</div>
        <div class="loop-arrow">→</div>
        <div class="loop-node">Sugar spike</div>
        <div class="loop-arrow">→</div>
        <div class="loop-node">Crash</div>
        <div class="loop-arrow">→</div>
        <div class="loop-node reset">Cortisol rises again</div>
        <div class="loop-arrow">↺</div>
      </div>
      <p class="loop-caption">This loop, if left uninterrupted, can affect your mood, focus, sleep, and long-term brain health. The good news: understanding the loop is the first step to breaking it.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- COOL FACT -->
  <section>
    <div class="section-label">Cool Fact · Mind = Blown</div>
    <h2>Your Gut Sends Stress Signals Straight to Your Brain</h2>
    <p>Your gut and brain are connected by a communication highway called the <strong>gut-brain axis</strong>, via the vagus nerve. About 90% of the serotonin in your body is actually produced in your gut — not your brain.</p>
    <div class="pull-quote">
      When you eat a high-sugar diet, it disrupts the balance of bacteria in your gut. This disruption reduces serotonin production and sends inflammatory signals up to the brain, which <strong>increase anxiety</strong> and make the stress response more reactive. Eating well is not just about your body — it is literally protecting your mental health from the inside out.
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- MYTH BUSTER -->
  <section>
    <div class="section-label">Myth Buster</div>
    <h2>Let's Set the Record Straight</h2>
    <div class="myth-grid">
      <div class="myth-col myth">
        <div class="col-label">✕ The Myth</div>
        <p>"Eating something sweet calms me down when I am stressed. It is just a comfort thing and it works." Many teens (and adults) treat sugary snacks as a reliable stress reliever — and it genuinely feels that way in the moment.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="myth-col truth">
        <div class="col-label">✓ The Truth</div>
        <p>The relief is real but it is <strong>temporary and followed by a cortisol rebound</strong>. Over time, using sugar to manage stress trains your brain to depend on it, raises your baseline cortisol, and increases anxiety. It feels like it works because dopamine spikes briefly. But the biology underneath is moving in the wrong direction.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
