  <!-- TRY THIS TODAY -->
  <section>
    <div class="section-label">Try This Today</div>
    <h2>Interrupt the Sugar-Stress Loop</h2>
    <p>The next time you feel stressed and reach for something sweet, try this instead. It is not about willpower — it is about giving your brain a better biological option.</p>

    <div class="exercise-steps">
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">1</div>
        <div class="step-text"><strong>Pause for 90 seconds.</strong> Research by neuroscientist Dr Jill Bolte Taylor shows that a cortisol wave in the brain lasts about 90 seconds. If you wait without feeding the craving, the intensity drops. Set a timer and breathe.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">2</div>
        <div class="step-text"><strong>Drink a glass of water first.</strong> As we covered in Issue 04, dehydration intensifies the stress response and sharpens cravings. Hydration is the lowest-effort reset available to you.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">3</div>
        <div class="step-text"><strong>If you are genuinely hungry, choose steady fuel.</strong> Reach for something with protein and complex carbohydrates — like groundnut soup and rice, eggs and bread, or a banana with peanut butter. These stabilise blood glucose rather than spike it.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">4</div>
        <div class="step-text"><strong>Move your body for five minutes.</strong> A short walk or stretching session lowers cortisol directly and raises BDNF, which supports calmer thinking. Recall from Issue 04 that even brief exercise shifts your brain chemistry.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">5</div>
        <div class="step-text"><strong>Notice the pattern without judgement.</strong> Keep a simple note on your phone for one week. When did you crave sugar? What was the stress trigger? Seeing the loop written down makes it easier to interrupt next time.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="why-box">
      <div class="why-icon">💡</div>
      <p><strong>Why it works:</strong> Each step targets a different part of the sugar-stress loop. The pause interrupts the automatic cortisol-driven craving. Water addresses dehydration that amplifies stress. Steady fuel prevents the blood sugar crash that re-triggers cortisol. Movement burns off the stress hormones directly. And noticing the pattern hands control back to your <strong>prefrontal cortex</strong> — the same brain region stress weakens and that steady habits rebuild.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

